PREDICT4 -- #530 closing pass 5, Opus seat (2nd instance): the COORDINATOR'S SIX-CELL derivation, received mid-run.
Written BEFORE RXW / Wdrop / Rdrop are built and BEFORE any of them runs.  This file's mtime is the order proof.

THE SIX CELLS, re-derived here from dev_lca.c rather than taken from the message.  The handler makes EXACTLY ONE
1-byte forward per access: the write arm's `return cpu->memory_rw(..., writeflag, ...)' and the read arm's
`cpu->memory_rw(..., MEM_READ, ...)'.  Over one forward there are three one-edit shapes -- DROP, DUPLICATE, CROSS --
and two arms.

  cell            edit                                     name       status before this pass
  write x DROP    `return 1;' for the forward               Wdrop      NEVER BUILT
  write x DUP     a second store forward                    WWall/WWtail/WW6   BUILT (this pass): tail forms ESCAPE 185/185
  write x CROSS   drop the `return' -> the read also runs   Wpure/W23/cdxZL3/dNZtail  in the contract (x_wfallthru)
  read  x DROP    delete the read forward                   Rdrop      NEVER BUILT
  read  x DUP     a second read forward                     RD2/RD2post/RD2z   in the contract (RD2)
  read  x CROSS   `MEM_READ' -> `MEM_WRITE' (ONE TOKEN)     RXW        NEVER BUILT

  I keep RWall (read arm + an ADDED MEM_WRITE, count 2 mixed) as a SEVENTH shape, measured earlier this pass at
  167/185.  It is not the same edit as RXW: RXW SWAPS the direction (count stays 1), RWall ADDS (count becomes 2).
  The coordinator's (c) is an ADD on the write arm and a SWAP on the read arm; both belong in the enumeration.

PREDICTIONS.  I predict FAMILIES and say where a number would be dishonest -- `byte' is UNINITIALISED in two of these
and its value is stack residue, so an exact count would be a guess dressed as a prediction.

  RXW     185-row probe: HEAVILY CAUGHT, not an escape.  The read no longer happens, so every DEVSIG/value read row
          (`live' 0x55, `ofs1 devsig' 0x60, `ofs2 devsig' 0xb0, `ofs3 devsig' 0x5a, `iir lca rd*', `cmos r*',
          `rdq r*', `tailr t*') should red unless stack residue happens to hold the wanted byte.  I will NOT state a
          score.  ** The claim I am testing is NOT whether it escapes -- it is WHETHER ANY ROW SEES THE WRITE. **
          The header says a DIRECTION swap is "a different class, caught by the value rows"; if every red is a value
          row then the detector catches RXW for the MISSING READ and nothing rows the ACQUIRED WRITE, and the
          value-preserving variant of it (RWall, already measured 167/185 stub-only) is the live gap.
          pitrows: `pl*_dlv' and `pl*_r1' (32+32) and `pw*_r1' (5) RED -- the lca "load" now writes the uninitialised
          byte into the counter LSB and never advances rd_msb; `ps*' GREEN (the write arm is untouched); `live*'
          GREEN.  Again no score: the delivered garbage may coincide with a want.
  Wdrop   185-row probe: HEAVILY CAUGHT.  `ofs3 devsig' (SCR store/read-back), `cmos w1-w8' (REG C never set -> 0x00
          not 0x0f), `kbcq * dib' and `tailq w *' (no command reaches the 8042, so no reply is queued -> DIB 0),
          `split'/`st4'/`w*' value rows.  I predict it is NOT an escape, which is the coordinator's guess too --
          and the point of building it is that a guess is not a measurement.
          NOTE: `iir st*' should stay GREEN -- its oracle is "TXRDY SURVIVES the store", and a store that never
          happens also lets TXRDY survive.  A row that is green both when the store lands and when it is dropped is
          the CHANNEL-line shape, and if that is what the log shows it is a finding about those rows.
  Rdrop   185-row probe: HEAVILY CAUGHT, same read-side family as RXW, no write side effect.  Build risk: `byte' is
          then genuinely uninitialised on the read path and the compiler may refuse it.  A BUILD_FAIL is a RESULT --
          it says the cell is not reachable by a one-edit mutant -- and will be recorded as one, not retried away.

WHAT WOULD CHANGE MY VERDICT: an escape (>= 184/185) on any of the three.  I do not expect one; I am measuring
because the enumeration is the thing under test and "it would be caught" is an inference, not a measurement.
